---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Lunar Nodes', href: '/explore/lunar-nodes/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Lunar Nodes, the North and South Node axis"
  description="The lunar nodes are the two points where the Moon's orbit crosses the ecliptic. Learn how astrologers interpret the nodal axis, how it relates to eclipses, and why it is better read as a relationship than two labels."
  path="/explore/lunar-nodes/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The lunar nodes</h1>
        <p class="lede">They are not planets at all, but two mathematical points created by the crossing of two orbital planes. I like that about them: something physically invisible can still describe a relationship in the geometry.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.whaleTailStars.src} srcset={ocean.whaleTailStars.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.whaleTailStars.alt} width={ocean.whaleTailStars.width} height={ocean.whaleTailStars.height} style="object-position: center 76%;" fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What they physically are</h2>
      <p>
        The Moon's orbit is tilted relative to the ecliptic. The two places where those planes
        intersect are the <strong>North Node</strong> and <strong>South Node</strong>. They are
        always opposite one another, so I find it more useful to think of them as one axis than as
        two separate placements.
      </p>
      <p>
        The nodes move backward through the zodiac over an approximately 18.6-year cycle. They are
        also central to the geometry of <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipses</a>: a solar or lunar
        eclipse occurs when a new or full moon falls close enough to this nodal axis.
      </p>

      <h2>How astrologers read them</h2>
      <p>
        Many modern astrologers interpret the South Node as familiar territory and the North Node
        as less familiar development. That language can be useful as long as it does not turn into a
        moral ranking in which one end of the axis is “bad” and the other is the destination you are
        supposed to reach.
      </p>
      <p>
        I prefer the relationship between them. The South Node can describe capacities, habits, or
        ways of operating that come more readily. The North Node can point toward qualities that ask
        for more conscious development. But the two work together. Growth rarely requires throwing
        away what you already know how to do.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the larger lesson I keep finding in charts: opposites are often not problems to solve
        by choosing one side. They are relationships to learn how to hold.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.moth} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Signs, houses, and aspects</h2>
      <p>
        The node's sign describes one layer of the symbolism; the house shows where that axis falls
        in the life, and that house depends on your birth time. Aspects from planets add still more
        information. A nodal placement becomes more useful as context accumulates, not as the label
        becomes more dramatic.
      </p>

      <h2>The collective layer</h2>
      <p>
        Because the nodes remain on the same sign axis for many months, large groups of people share
        the same nodal signs. That is another reason sign-only interpretations cannot be very
        personal. The distinction comes from where the axis falls in an individual chart and what it
        connects with there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Related: <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipses</a>,
        <a href="/explore/transits/">transits and timing</a>, and
        <a href="/explore/personal-purpose/">what a chart can suggest about direction</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Want to follow the nodal axis into the sky itself?"
    body="Eclipses are where the nodes become visible in the larger cycle. Start there, then use Current Sky to see when those patterns are active now."
    buttonLabel="Explore Eclipses"
    buttonHref="/explore/eclipses/"
    secondaryLabel="See Current Sky"
    secondaryHref="/current-sky/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
